The Commission tries to keep every voter within about 2 km of a booth. That leads to some extraordinary journeys. Officials have walked for days into the hills of Arunachal Pradesh, crossed rivers by boat in Assam and ridden in helicopters to islands. In Gir forest in Gujarat, a booth is set up for a single voter, a priest who lives near a temple among the lions.

At Tashigang in Himachal Pradesh, about 4,650 m above sea level, sits one of the highest polling stations in the world. The air there is so thin that the polling team has to watch for altitude sickness.

{{story:polls-elections}}

Indian farming has two main seasons. *Kharif* crops, such as rice, cotton and maize, are sown when the [[@monsoon|monsoon]] rains arrive in June or July and harvested in autumn. *Rabi* crops, such as wheat, mustard and gram, are sown in winter and harvested in spring.

When the rains come late or too heavy, whole harvests can fail. That is why farmers watch the sky and listen closely to weather forecasts. Many now get alerts and advice on their phones in their own language, including tips on when to spray, sow or harvest.

{{story:green-revolution}}

In the 1920s, the German mathematician David Hilbert described a strange hotel with infinitely many rooms, all full. A new guest arrives. The manager asks every guest to move from room n to room n + 1. Everyone still has a room, and room 1 is now free.

Even an infinite busload of new guests can be fitted in. Each current guest moves to the room with double their number, filling all the even rooms. The odd rooms are now empty, and there are infinitely many of them. With infinity, "full" does not mean what it usually does.

{{story:big-bang}}

Before it went on air, researchers showed test episodes to groups of young children. They noted the moments when the children looked away. Early on, scenes with only human actors didn't hold attention well, so the makers mixed in more Muppets.

The show was built like a TV advert, with short, punchy segments. One might teach the letter B, another the number 7, another how to share. Many episodes are "brought to you by" a letter and a number, a joke about real adverts.

{{story:puppetry}}

ज़्यादातर एलर्जी बस परेशान करती हैं: छींकें, आँखों में खुजली, दाने। कुछ लोगों में प्रतिक्रिया पूरे शरीर में फैल जाती है, जिसे एनाफ़िलैक्सिस कहते हैं। उनका गला या होंठ सूज सकते हैं, साँस लेना मुश्किल हो जाता है और वे बेहोश हो सकते हैं। इसमें तुरंत आपात मदद चाहिए।

जिन लोगों को पता है कि उन्हें गंभीर एलर्जी है, वे अक्सर एड्रेनालिन पेन साथ रखते हैं, एक छोटा इंजेक्शन जिसे सेकंडों में लगाया जा सकता है। भारत और अमेरिका के बहुत से स्कूल अब शिक्षकों को इसके लक्षण पहचानना सिखाते हैं। मूँगफली, मेवों, दूध, अंडों और सीप-झींगों से होने वाली [[@food-allergies|खाने की एलर्जी]] सबसे आम गंभीर एलर्जियों में हैं।

{{story:food-allergies}}

हर रेशे के अंदर उससे भी पतले लाखों धागे होते हैं, जो दो [[protein|प्रोटीनों]] से बने हैं, एक्टिन और मायोसिन। 1954 में ब्रिटेन के वैज्ञानिकों ने पाया कि ये धागे सिकुड़ते नहीं, बल्कि एक-दूसरे के पास से सरकते हैं। मायोसिन के नन्हे-नन्हे सिर होते हैं जो एक्टिन को पकड़ते हैं, खींचते हैं, छोड़ते हैं और फिर पकड़ते हैं, बिल्कुल रस्साकशी की टीम की तरह जो हाथ-दर-हाथ रस्सी खींचती है।

हर पकड़ में खाने से मिली थोड़ी [[energy|ऊर्जा]] लगती है। इसीलिए माँसपेशियों को खून से ऑक्सीजन और शक्कर की लगातार सप्लाई चाहिए, और इसीलिए [[@heartbeat-exercise|कसरत]] करते समय दिल तेज़ धड़कता है।

{{story:lever-lift}}

Perkin decided his purple was more exciting than his failed medicine. Against his teacher's advice, he left college, patented the dye at 18 and, with his father's savings, built a factory at Greenford near London in 1857.

At first, dyers weren't sure. Then fashion helped. In 1858, Queen Victoria wore a mauve gown to her daughter's wedding. In France, Empress Eugénie loved the colour too. Soon mauve dresses, ribbons and gloves were everywhere. The craze was so strong that the magazine *Punch* joked about "mauve measles" spreading across London.

{{story:inventions-accidents}}

Greek mathematicians loved proof: showing step by step why something must be true. Around 300 BCE, [[@euclid|Euclid]] wrote *Elements*, a [[geometry|geometry]] book used in schools for over 2,000 years.

Eratosthenes, a librarian in Alexandria, measured Earth's size using shadows. His answer was close to the real distance around the planet, about 40,000 km. Others studied the stars, [[@lever-lift|levers]] and the human body. Hippocrates, often called the father of medicine, taught doctors to look for natural causes of illness, not curses.

{{story:euclid}}

The Chinese emperor had banned opium, but smuggling went on. In 1839 he sent a tough official, Lin Zexu, to the southern port of Canton (now Guangzhou). Lin blocked foreign merchants inside their warehouses until they handed over their opium: more than 20,000 chests of it.

He destroyed it all at Humen. He even wrote a letter to Queen Victoria asking how Britain would feel if another country sold poison to its people. There is no sign she ever read it.

{{story:tea}}

By the 1770s, many colonists were angry at British taxes passed by a Parliament in London where they had no representatives. Their slogan was "no taxation without representation". On the night of 16 December 1773, a group of protesters boarded three ships and dumped 342 chests of [[@tea|tea]] into Boston Harbour.

Britain responded by closing the port. Tensions grew, and in April 1775 the first fighting of the Revolutionary War broke out near Boston, at Lexington and Concord. The night before, Paul Revere rode out to warn that British troops were coming. Today, the Freedom Trail, a red line painted and set in brick along the pavement, leads visitors about 4 km (2.5 miles) past 16 historic sites.

{{story:american-revolution}}

Green rules are written into how Singapore is built. Developers often have to replace any greenery they remove, on rooftops, walls or balconies. Some [[@skyscrapers|skyscrapers]] have gardens growing up their sides. A network of park connectors lets people walk and cycle between parks.

Gardens by the Bay, opened in 2012, has "Supertrees", tall metal structures 25 to 50 m high covered in living plants. Some collect rainwater and some carry [[@solar-panels|solar panels]]. Nearby, two huge glass domes hold plants from cool mountains and dry lands. Patches of real [[@rainforests|rainforest]] still survive on the island, too, along with [[@mangroves|mangroves]] on the coast.

{{story:water-purify}}

The word "waza" means a cook, and "wan" is often said to mean a shop or the cook's spread of dishes. The head chef is called the vasta waza. Wazas are usually men from families who have cooked for generations, learning recipes by watching rather than from books.

Kashmiri tradition says the wazas' ancestors came from Central Asia centuries ago, possibly from around Samarkand. Kashmir lay near routes connected to the [[@silk-road|Silk Road]], so cooks, traders and ideas did travel. Historians can't confirm the exact story, but Central Asian and [[@ancient-persia|Persian influences]] are clear in the food.

{{story:silk-road}}
